      <h2 id="nw-ovn-kubernetes-list-southbound-database-contents_ovn-kubernetes-architecture" class="group scroll-mt-20">Listing the OVN-Kubernetes southbound database contents<a href="#nw-ovn-kubernetes-list-southbound-database-contents_ovn-kubernetes-architecture" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">To understand OVN-Kubernetes (OVN-K) logical networking entities, examine the southbound database running as a container inside the <code>ovnkube-node</code> pod. Each node is controlled by the <code>ovnkube-controller</code> container running in that pod on the node.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>Access to the cluster as a user with the <code>cluster-admin</code> role.</li>
      <li>The OpenShift CLI (<code>oc</code>) is installed.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      List the pods in the network namespace by entering the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get po -n openshift-ovn-kubernetes"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                                     READY   STATUS    RESTARTS      AGE\novnkube-control-plane-8444dff7f9-4lh9k   2/2     Running   0             27m\novnkube-control-plane-8444dff7f9-5rjh9   2/2     Running   0             27m\novnkube-node-55xs2                       8/8     Running   0             26m\novnkube-node-7r84r                       8/8     Running   0             16m\novnkube-node-bqq8p                       8/8     Running   0             17m\novnkube-node-mkj4f                       8/8     Running   0             26m\novnkube-node-mlr8k                       8/8     Running   0             26m\novnkube-node-wqn2m                       8/8     Running   0             16m"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Optional: To list the pods with node information, enter the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get pods -n openshift-ovn-kubernetes -owide"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                                     READY   STATUS    RESTARTS      AGE   IP           NODE                                       NOMINATED NODE   READINESS GATES\novnkube-control-plane-8444dff7f9-4lh9k   2/2     Running   0             27m   10.0.0.3     ci-ln-t487nnb-72292-mdcnq-master-1         <none>           <none>\novnkube-control-plane-8444dff7f9-5rjh9   2/2     Running   0             27m   10.0.0.4     ci-ln-t487nnb-72292-mdcnq-master-2         <none>           <none>\novnkube-node-55xs2                       8/8     Running   0             26m   10.0.0.4     ci-ln-t487nnb-72292-mdcnq-master-2         <none>           <none>\novnkube-node-7r84r                       8/8     Running   0             17m   10.0.128.3   ci-ln-t487nnb-72292-mdcnq-worker-b-wbz7z   <none>           <none>\novnkube-node-bqq8p                       8/8     Running   0             17m   10.0.128.2   ci-ln-t487nnb-72292-mdcnq-worker-a-lh7ms   <none>           <none>\novnkube-node-mkj4f                       8/8     Running   0             27m   10.0.0.5     ci-ln-t487nnb-72292-mdcnq-master-0         <none>           <none>\novnkube-node-mlr8k                       8/8     Running   0             27m   10.0.0.3     ci-ln-t487nnb-72292-mdcnq-master-1         <none>           <none>\novnkube-node-wqn2m                       8/8     Running   0             17m   10.0.128.4   ci-ln-t487nnb-72292-mdcnq-worker-c-przlm   <none>           <none>"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>Open a remote shell into the <code>nbdb</code> or <code>sbdb</code> containers on the relevant node to examine the southbound database.</li>
      <li>
      Show all the objects in the southbound database by entering the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ ovn-sbctl show"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"Chassis \"5db31703-35e9-413b-8cdf-69e7eecb41f7\"\n    hostname: ci-ln-9gp362t-72292-v2p94-worker-a-8bmwz\n    Encap geneve\n        ip: \"10.0.128.4\"\n        options: {csum=\"true\"}\n    Port_Binding tstor-ci-ln-9gp362t-72292-v2p94-worker-a-8bmwz\nChassis \"070debed-99b7-4bce-b17d-17e720b7f8bc\"\n    hostname: ci-ln-9gp362t-72292-v2p94-worker-b-svmp6\n    Encap geneve\n        ip: \"10.0.128.2\"\n        options: {csum=\"true\"}\n    Port_Binding k8s-ci-ln-9gp362t-72292-v2p94-worker-b-svmp6\n    Port_Binding rtoe-GR_ci-ln-9gp362t-72292-v2p94-worker-b-svmp6\n    Port_Binding openshift-monitoring_alertmanager-main-1\n    Port_Binding rtoj-GR_ci-ln-9gp362t-72292-v2p94-worker-b-svmp6\n    Port_Binding etor-GR_ci-ln-9gp362t-72292-v2p94-worker-b-svmp6\n    Port_Binding cr-rtos-ci-ln-9gp362t-72292-v2p94-worker-b-svmp6\n    Port_Binding openshift-e2e-loki_loki-promtail-qcrcz\n    Port_Binding jtor-GR_ci-ln-9gp362t-72292-v2p94-worker-b-svmp6\n    Port_Binding openshift-multus_network-metrics-daemon-mkd4t\n    Port_Binding openshift-ingress-canary_ingress-canary-xtvj4\n    Port_Binding openshift-ingress_router-default-6c76cbc498-pvlqk\n    Port_Binding openshift-dns_dns-default-zz582\n    Port_Binding openshift-monitoring_thanos-querier-57585899f5-lbf4f\n    Port_Binding openshift-network-diagnostics_network-check-target-tn228\n    Port_Binding openshift-monitoring_prometheus-k8s-0\n    Port_Binding openshift-image-registry_image-registry-68899bd877-xqxjj\nChassis \"179ba069-0af1-401c-b044-e5ba90f60fea\"\n    hostname: ci-ln-9gp362t-72292-v2p94-master-0\n    Encap geneve\n        ip: \"10.0.0.5\"\n        options: {csum=\"true\"}\n    Port_Binding tstor-ci-ln-9gp362t-72292-v2p94-master-0\nChassis \"68c954f2-5a76-47be-9e84-1cb13bd9dab9\"\n    hostname: ci-ln-9gp362t-72292-v2p94-worker-c-mjf9w\n    Encap geneve\n        ip: \"10.0.128.3\"\n        options: {csum=\"true\"}\n    Port_Binding tstor-ci-ln-9gp362t-72292-v2p94-worker-c-mjf9w\nChassis \"2de65d9e-9abf-4b6e-a51d-a1e038b4d8af\"\n    hostname: ci-ln-9gp362t-72292-v2p94-master-2\n    Encap geneve\n        ip: \"10.0.0.4\"\n        options: {csum=\"true\"}\n    Port_Binding tstor-ci-ln-9gp362t-72292-v2p94-master-2\nChassis \"1d371cb8-5e21-44fd-9025-c4b162cc4247\"\n    hostname: ci-ln-9gp362t-72292-v2p94-master-1\n    Encap geneve\n        ip: \"10.0.0.3\"\n        options: {csum=\"true\"}\n    Port_Binding tstor-ci-ln-9gp362t-72292-v2p94-master-1"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <ul>
      <li>This detailed output shows the chassis and the ports that are attached to the chassis which in this case are all of the router ports and anything that uses host networking.</li>
      <li>Any pods communicate out to the wider network by using source network address translation (SNAT). Their IP address is translated into the IP address of the node that the pod is running on and then sent out into the network.</li>
      <li>In addition to the chassis information the southbound database has all the logic flows and those logic flows are then sent to the <code>ovn-controller</code> running on each of the nodes. The <code>ovn-controller</code> translates the logic flows into open flow rules and ultimately programs <code>OpenvSwitch</code> so that your pods can then follow open flow rules and make it out of the network.</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Display the options available with the <code>ovn-sbctl</code> command by entering the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc exec -n openshift-ovn-kubernetes -it ovnkube-node-55xs2 \\\n-c sbdb ovn-sbctl --help"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="nw-ovn-kubernetes-examine-sb-database-contents-ref_ovn-kubernetes-architecture" class="group scroll-mt-20">Command-line arguments for ovn-sbctl to examine southbound database contents<a href="#nw-ovn-kubernetes-examine-sb-database-contents-ref_ovn-kubernetes-architecture" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>The following table describes the command-line arguments that can be used with <code>ovn-sbctl</code> to examine the contents of the southbound database.</p>
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>Open a remote shell in the pod you wish to view the contents of and then run the <code>ovn-sbctl</code> commands.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <p><strong>Command-line arguments to examine southbound database contents</strong></p>
      <Table class="my-4">
      <TableHeader>
      <TableRow>
      <TableHead>Argument</TableHead>
      <TableHead>Description</TableHead>
      </TableRow>
      </TableHeader>
      <TableBody>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>ovn-sbctl show</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>An overview of the southbound database contents as seen from a specific node.</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>ovn-sbctl list Port_Binding &lt;port&gt;</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>List the contents of southbound database for a the specified port .</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>ovn-sbctl dump-flows</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>List the logical flows.</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      </TableBody>
      </Table>
      <h2 id="ovn-kubernetes-logical-architecture-con_ovn-kubernetes-architecture" class="group scroll-mt-20">OVN-Kubernetes logical architecture<a href="#ovn-kubernetes-logical-architecture-con_ovn-kubernetes-architecture" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">OVN-Kubernetes is a network virtualization solution that creates logical switches and routers across cluster nodes. These virtual infrastructure components interconnect to construct distinct network topologies.</p>
      <p><strong>Figure 1. OVN-Kubernetes router and switch components</strong></p>
      <BlockImage src="/openshift-docs-markdown/_assets/images/299_OpenShift_OVN-Kubernetes_arch_1023_2.png" alt="OVN-Kubernetes logical architecture" />
      <p>The key components involved in packet processing are:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold">Gateway routers</dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Gateway routers sometimes called L3 gateway routers, are typically used between the distributed routers and the physical network. Gateway routers including their logical patch ports are bound to a physical location (not distributed), or chassis. The patch ports on this router are known as l3gateway ports in the ovn-southbound database (<code>ovn-sbdb</code>).</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold">Distributed logical routers</dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Distributed logical routers and the logical switches behind them, to which virtual machines and containers attach, effectively reside on each hypervisor.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold">Join local switch</dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Join local switches are used to connect the distributed router and gateway routers. It reduces the number of IP addresses needed on the distributed router.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold">Logical switches with patch ports</dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Logical switches with patch ports virtualize the network stack. These components connect remote logical ports through network encapsulation tunnels.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold">Logical switches with localnet ports</dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Logical switches with localnet ports connect the OVN layout to the physical network. These elements connect remote logical ports by bridging packets to directly attached physical Layer 2 segments.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold">Patch ports</dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Patch ports provide connectivity between logical switches and logical routers, or between peer logical routers. A single connection uses a pair of patch ports at each connectivity intersection, one on each side.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>l3gateway</code> ports</dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Port binding entries in the <code>ovn-sbdb</code> for logical patch ports used in gateway routers. They are called <code>l3gateway</code> ports rather than patch ports because these ports are bound to a chassis similar to the gateway router itself.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold">localnet ports</dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>localnet ports are present on the bridged logical switches that allows a connection to a locally accessible network from each <code>ovn-controller</code> instance. This helps model the direct connectivity to the physical network from the logical switches. A logical switch can only have a single localnet port attached to it.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>Executing <code>ovnkube-trace</code> with the log level configuration set to <code>2</code> or <code>5</code> exposes these internal OVN-Kubernetes logical routing components for analysis.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <h3 id="nw-ovn-kubernetes-installing-network-tools_ovn-kubernetes-architecture" class="group scroll-mt-20">Installing network-tools on local host<a href="#nw-ovn-kubernetes-installing-network-tools_ovn-kubernetes-architecture" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p class="_abstract">Install <code>network-tools</code> on your local host for debugging OpenShift Container Platform cluster network issues.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Clone the <code>network-tools</code> repository onto your workstation with the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ git clone git@github.com:openshift/network-tools.git"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Change into the directory for the repository you just cloned:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ cd network-tools"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Optional: List all available commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ ./debug-scripts/network-tools -h"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h3 id="nw-ovn-kubernetes-running-network-tools_ovn-kubernetes-architecture" class="group scroll-mt-20">Running network-tools<a href="#nw-ovn-kubernetes-running-network-tools_ovn-kubernetes-architecture" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p class="_abstract">Run <code>network-tools</code> to retrieve configuration status from logical switches and routers when diagnosing internal routing loops and cluster network issues.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You installed the OpenShift CLI (<code>oc</code>).</li>
      <li>You are logged in to the cluster as a user with <code>cluster-admin</code> privileges.</li>
      <li>You have installed <code>network-tools</code> on local host.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Display the logical router map by entering the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ ./debug-scripts/network-tools ovn-db-run-command ovn-nbctl lr-list"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"944a7b53-7948-4ad2-a494-82b55eeccf87 (GR_ci-ln-54932yb-72292-kd676-worker-c-rzj99)\n84bd4a4c-4b0b-4a47-b0cf-a2c32709fc53 (ovn_cluster_router)"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Locate active port bindings with local network attributes by entering the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ ./debug-scripts/network-tools ovn-db-run-command \"ovn-sbctl find Port_Binding type=localnet\""} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"_uuid               : d05298f5-805b-4838-9224-1211afc2f199\nadditional_chassis  : []\nadditional_encap    : []\nchassis             : []\ndatapath            : f3c2c959-743b-4037-854d-26627902597c\nencap               : []\nexternal_ids        : {}\ngateway_chassis     : []\nha_chassis_group    : []\nlogical_port        : br-ex_ci-ln-54932yb-72292-kd676-worker-c-rzj99\nmac                 : [unknown]\nmirror_rules        : []\nnat_addresses       : []\noptions             : {network_name=physnet}\nparent_port         : []\nport_security       : []\nrequested_additional_chassis: []\nrequested_chassis   : []\ntag                 : []\ntunnel_key          : 2\ntype                : localnet\nup                  : false\nvirtual_parent      : []\n\n[...]"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
